U_1, \\Lambda) \\to H_\\etale^i(U_0\\cap U_1,\n\\Lambda).\n$$", "MATH_0008": "$X$", "MATH_0009": "$\\mathbf{C}$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathbf{A}^1_\\mathbf{C}$", "MATH_0011": "$H_\\etale^1 (\\mathbf{A}^1_\\mathbf{C}, \\mathbf{Z}/n\\mathbf{Z}) = 0$", "MATH_0012": "$\\varphi : Y \\to \\mathbf{A}^1_\\mathbf{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}$", "MATH_0013": "$Y$", "MATH_0014": "$Y$", "MATH_0015": "$n$", "MATH_0016": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\n{Y\\ } \\ar@{^{(}->}[r] \\ar[d]^\\varphi &\n{\\bar Y} \\ar[d]^{\\bar\\varphi} \\\\\n{\\mathbf{A}^1_\\mathbf{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}} \\ar@{^{(}->}[r] &\n{\\mathbf{P}^1_\\mathbf{C}}\n}\n$$", "MATH_0017": "$\\varphi$", "MATH_0018": "$\\bar{\\varphi}$", "MATH_0019": "$\\infty$", "MATH_0020": "$y_1,\n\\ldots, y_r$", "MATH_0021": "$e_1, \\ldots e_r$", "MATH_0022": "$\\infty$", "MATH_0023": "$y_1', \\ldots, y_s'$", "MATH_0024": "$d_1, \\ldots d_s$", "MATH_0025": "$\\sum e_i = n = \\sum d_j$", "MATH_0026": "$$\n2 g_Y - 2 = -2n + \\sum (e_i - 1) + \\sum (d_j - 1)\n$$", "MATH_0027": "$g_Y = 0$", "MATH_0028": "$r = s = 1$", "MATH_0029": "$e_1 = d_1 = n$", "MATH_0030": "$Y \\cong {\\mathbf{A}^1_\\mathbf{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}}$", "MATH_0031": "$\\varphi(z) = \\lambda z^n$", "MATH_0032": "$\\lambda \\in \\mathbf{C}^*$", "MATH_0033": "$Y$", "MATH_0034": "$\\lambda = 1$", "MATH_0035": "$n$", "MATH_0036": "$\\mathbf{A}^1_{\\mathbf{C}} \\setminus \\{0\\}$", "MATH_0037": "${\\mathbf{A}^1_\\mathbf{C} \\setminus \\{0\\}}$", "MATH_0038": "$\\mathbf{Z}/n\\mathbf{Z}$", "MATH_0039": "$Y$", "MATH_0040": "$z$", "MATH_0041": "$\\zeta_n z$", "MATH_0042": "$\\zeta_n$", "MATH_0043": "$n$", "MATH_0044": "$\\phi(n)$", "MATH_0045": "$\\phi(n)$", "MATH_0046": "$\\phi(n)$", "MATH_0047": "$\\mathbf{Z}/n\\mathbf{Z}$", "MATH_0048": "$n$", "MATH_0049": "$n$", "MATH_0050": "$\\mathbf{A}^1_{\\mathbf{C}} \\setminus \\{0\\}$", "MATH_0051": "$\\mathbf{Z}/n\\mathbf{Z}$", "MATH_0052": "$n$", "MATH_0053": "$1$", "MATH_0054": "$$\nH_\\etale^1 (\\mathbf{A}^1_\\mathbf{C} \\setminus \\{0\\},\n\\mathbf{Z}/n\\mathbf{Z}) =\n\\Hom(\\mu_n(\\mathbf{C}), \\mathbf{Z}/n\\mathbf{Z}) \\cong \\mathbf{Z}/n\\mathbf{Z}.\n$$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{remark-normalize-H1-Gm}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0002": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0003": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0004": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0005": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0006": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0007": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0008": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0009": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0010": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0011": "\\noindent", "TEXTBFCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTBFCLOSE_0002": "}", "TEXTBFOPEN_0001": "{\\bf ", "TEXTBFOPEN_0002": "{\\bf "}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 3, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:dd2b0ecdce847675bb0576fbfab1cf0ec80756243a7dd108ec800af3b966cd7b", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:65740fe930e3cc3089a3f95f899b3c28fe7649766e1db099664cc0da5f45a2e2", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:980d067e7fc03efd16cbda7ec8fe48cc791cc115db58853ad49d1129b98f82de", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nHow do we compute the cohomology of <MATH_0001> with coefficients\n<MATH_0002>?\nWe use <GROUPOPEN_0001><ACCENT_0001><GROUPCLOSE_0001>ech cohomology. A covering of <MATH_0003> is given\nby the two standard opens <MATH_0004>, which are both\nisomorphic to <MATH_0005>, and whose intersection is isomorphic\nto <MATH_0006>.\nIt turns out that the Mayer-Vietoris sequence holds in <ACCENT_0002>tale cohomology.\nThis gives an exact sequence\n<MATH_0007>\nTo get the answer we expect, we would need to show that the direct sum in the\nthird term vanishes. In fact, it is true that, as for the usual topology,\n<MATHSEG_0001> for <MATHSEG_0002>\nand\n<MATHSEG_0003> if <MATHSEG_0004>, and<MATHSEG_0005> for <MATHSEG_0006>\nThese results are already quite hard (what is an elementary proof?). Let us\nexplain how we would compute this once the machinery of <ACCENT_0003>tale cohomology is\nat our disposal.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\n<TEXTBFOPEN_0001>Higher cohomology.<TEXTBFCLOSE_0001> This is taken care of by the following general\nfact: if <MATH_0008> is an affine curve over <MATH_0009>, then\n<MATHSEG_0007> for <MATHSEG_0008>\nThis is proved by considering the generic point of the curve and doing some\nGalois cohomology. So we only have to worry about the cohomology in degree 1.\n\n<STRUCT_0004><STRUCT_0005>\n<TEXTBFOPEN_0002>Cohomology in degree 1.<TEXTBFCLOSE_0002> We use the following identifications:\n<MATHSEG_0009>sheaves of sets <MATHSEG_0010> on the <MATHSEG_0011>tale site <MATHSEG_0012> endowed with an<MATHSEG_0013>action <MATHSEG_0014> such that <MATHSEG_0015> is a <MATHSEG_0016>-torsor.<MATHSEG_0017>morphisms <MATHSEG_0018> which are finite <MATHSEG_0019>tale together<MATHSEG_0020> with a free <MATHSEG_0021> action such that <MATHSEG_0022>\nThe first identification is very general (it is true for any cohomology theory\non a site) and has nothing to do with the <ACCENT_0004>tale topology. The second\nidentification is a consequence of descent theory. The last set describes a\ncollection of geometric objects on which we can get our hands.\n\n<STRUCT_0006><STRUCT_0007>\nThe curve <MATH_0010> has no nontrivial finite <ACCENT_0005>tale covering\nand hence\n<MATH_0011>.\nThis can be seen either topologically or by using the argument in the next\nparagraph.\n\n<STRUCT_0008><STRUCT_0009>\nLet us describe the finite <ACCENT_0006>tale coverings\n<MATH_0012>.\nIt suffices to consider the case where <MATH_0013> is\nconnected, which we assume. We are going to find out what <MATH_0014> can be\nby applying the Riemann-Hurwitz formula (of course this is a bit silly, and\nyou can go ahead and skip the next section if you like).\nSay that this morphism is <MATH_0015> to 1, and consider a\nprojective compactification\n<MATH_0016>\nEven though <MATH_0017> is <ACCENT_0007>tale and does not ramify, <MATH_0018> may\nramify at 0 and <MATH_0019>. Say that the preimages of 0 are the points <MATH_0020> with indices of ramification <MATH_0021>, and that the\npreimages of <MATH_0022> are the points <MATH_0023> with indices of\nramification <MATH_0024>. In particular, <MATH_0025>.\nApplying the Riemann-Hurwitz formula, we get\n<MATH_0026>\nand therefore <MATH_0027>, <MATH_0028> and <MATH_0029>.\nHence <MATH_0030>, and it is easy to\nsee that <MATH_0031> for some <MATH_0032>.\nAfter reparametrizing <MATH_0033> we may assume <MATH_0034>. Thus our\ncovering is given by taking the <MATH_0035>th root of the coordinate on\n<MATH_0036>.\n\n<STRUCT_0010><STRUCT_0011>\nRemember that we need to classify the coverings of\n<MATH_0037> together with free\n<MATH_0038>-actions on them.\nIn our case any such action corresponds\nto an automorphism of <MATH_0039> sending <MATH_0040> to <MATH_0041>, where <MATH_0042> is a\nprimitive <MATH_0043>th root of unity. There are <MATH_0044> such actions\n(here <MATH_0045> means the Euler function). Thus there are exactly\n<MATH_0046> connected finite <ACCENT_0008>tale coverings with a given free\n<MATH_0047>-action, each corresponding to a primitive\n<MATH_0048>th root of unity. We leave it to the reader to see that the\ndisconnected finite <ACCENT_0009>tale degree <MATH_0049> coverings of\n<MATH_0050> with a given free\n<MATH_0051>-action correspond one-to-one with <MATH_0052>th\nroots of <MATH_0053> which are not primitive.\nIn other words, this computation shows that\n<MATH_0054>\nThe first identification is canonical, the second isn't, see\nRemark <REF_0001>.\nSince the proof of Riemann-Hurwitz does not use the computation of\ncohomology, the above actually constitutes a proof (provided we\nfill in the details on vanishing, etc).\n\n\n\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:2b9ca7997882bc727d85c84b16fac1969821f581aed58f3513e3ac4d17591783", "unit_id": "tag:03N8:prose-0001"}
